# New repository defaults (core)

Author: Pandiyaraj Karuppasamy
Date: Nov-14-2026

Owner rule (Nov-14-2026): **always prefer a private repo and the `main` branch.**

## Private by default

- Create every GitHub repository as **private**: `gh repo create <owner>/<name> --private`.
  Never pass `--public`, and never omit the flag (an interactive default can pick public).
- Make a repo public only when the owner asks for it in so many words for that repo
  (for example a PyPI package page or a GitHub Pages site). Even then, prefer publishing a
  slice (see the `publish-course-static-site` skill) over flipping the source repo.
- A public repo cannot be made fully private again: forks, clones and caches survive the
  switch. Treat going public as irreversible and confirm first.

## `main`, never `master`

- `git init -b main` (git 2.28+). The global config carries `init.defaultBranch=main`, but
  pass `-b main` anyway so a machine without that config still gets it right.
- `hubctl init` uses `git init -b main`. A scaffold that still comes out on `master` gets
  `git branch -m master main` **before** the first push, so the remote never has `master`.
- `gh repo create --source . --push` takes the current local branch as the default branch,
  so rename first, push second.

## Check after the first push

```bash
gh repo view <owner>/<name> --json visibility,defaultBranchRef \
  --jq '.visibility + " " + .defaultBranchRef.name'     # expect: PRIVATE main
```

If either is wrong: `gh repo edit <owner>/<name> --visibility private --accept-visibility-change-consequences`,
and `gh repo edit <owner>/<name> --default-branch main` after pushing `main`, then delete the
remote `master`.

Use the **`github-repo-create`** skill for the full sequence.
